    "q-18236": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18236",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-k$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =-k[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k+16\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-k \\\\ -5k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=-k, \\\\ 3k+16=-5k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2=-k\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=-2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k+16 & =-5k \\\\ 3\\cdot (-2)+16 & =-5\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 10 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
