    "q-107312": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107312",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n\nII. An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nIII. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =15.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{3}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $3$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=3v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(5v)=5Av=5(3v)=3(5v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(5v)=3(5v),$ and $5v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $5v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
